  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front and associated upper level trough will be very\nslowly moving eastward from the Great Lakes and into the\nNortheast for Sunday into Monday. Ahead of this boundary, it\nwill be very moist across the region, with surface dewpoints\nwell into the 60s, with some low 70s in valley areas. PWAT\nvalues will be exceeding two inches in many areas by early\nMonday and these values are 2-3 STD above normal. Guidance\nsuggests rounds of showers and embedded thunderstorms will start\nimpacting northwestern areas by later Sunday, with the highest\ncoverage across the area for Sunday night through Monday \nafternoon. \n\nModel soundings shows a low of ingredients that would favor the\npotential for heavy downpours and possible flash flooding. In \naddition to the high PWATs, there will be a moist profile \nthroughout the entire troposphere, high freezing levels and a \nnarrow skinny CAPE profile. This would result in highly \nefficient rainfall processes, allowing for heavy downpours. \n\nThere has been some question about where the heaviest rainfall\nwill set up and some of the guidance has helped put this into\nbetter focus. Based on the latest info, northwestern areas\n(western Adirondacks) may see some of the heaviest downpours\ninitially for late Sunday into Sunday night. For Monday, it\nappears far southern and southeastern areas may be most at risk,\nwith the heaviest totals perhaps just south of the area into the\nnorthern mid Atlantic States. While the NBM suggests that there\nis a decent chance for a widespread 1\" of rainfall (over 50\npercent for many areas), it is not showing too high potential\nfor over 2\" (with under 20% for most of the region). Still, hi-\nres guidance, including the 12z SPC HREF, does suggest the\npotential for locally 2-3\" in some areas that see repeated\nbursts of heavy rainfall for western and southern areas. The\nareas most at risk will be locations that saw heavy rainfall\nearlier in the week, as soils remain saturated and FFG is rather\nlow for these areas. While far southern areas (such as near\nPoughkeepsie) are still in a rainfall deficit, locations not\nfar away across northern parts of the mid Hudson Vally are\nfairly wet, so will need to closely monitor radar trends Sunday\ninto Monday once showers and thunderstorms develop. There is \ncurrently a marginal risk for flash flooding for Sunday night \ninto Monday for most of the area. Far southern areas (Dutchess\nand Litchfield Counties) also reach into the Slight Risk, which\nsuggests the potential for isolated to scattered flash \nflooding.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will be winding down by Monday\nevening, as the best forcing and moisture shifts eastward, which\nwill end the potential for any additional flooding by that time.",
